Mark McKinzie writes
> Spoiler follows!
> 
> Did you know you can answer
>      Which sorts of things would you like identified[)[("+= a]?
> with more than one argument?  So e.g. if we do
> 
> Which sorts of things would you like identified[)[("+= a]?   =+()["
> 
> it will first do your ring(s), then if it can continue, it will
> continue onto the spellbooks, then the tools, etc...

Furthermore, if you want to ID first the uncursed rings, then the uncursed  
wands, then the cursed rings, then Excalibur, then the cursed wands, then  
the rest of the weapons, you can reply:

	=/=)/)

In other words, you can do multiple types multiple times. The first time  
through the rings, ID only the ones you care about most; if you're lucky  
enough to go through them a second time, you can ID the rest of them.

A point of clarification: an uncursed scroll of identify also has a chance  
to identify more than one item, and a blessed scroll merely has a better  
chance. Thus it helps to prioritize your unidentified items using the  
above method for all types of ID scrolls, cause you never know if you'll  
get a super scroll.

